A **horizontal** line is parallel to the $x$-axis, and its equation $y = c$ says every point on it has $y$-coordinate $c$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\usetikzlibrary{arrows.meta}
\draw[gray!30,very thin] (-3,-3) grid (3,3);
\draw[{Stealth}-{Stealth}] (-3.4,0) -- (3.4,0) node[below] {$x$};
\draw[{Stealth}-{Stealth}] (0,-3.4) -- (0,3.4) node[left] {$y$};
\draw[thick,{Stealth}-{Stealth}] (-3.2,2) -- (3.2,2);
\node[right] at (3.35,2) {$y=c$};
\fill (0,2) circle (2pt);
\node[anchor=north east] at (-0.08,1.92) {$c$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

A **vertical** line differs only in direction: it is parallel to the $y$-axis, and its equation $x = k$ says every point has $x$-coordinate $k$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\usetikzlibrary{arrows.meta}
\draw[gray!30,very thin] (-3,-3) grid (3,3);
\draw[{Stealth}-{Stealth}] (-3.4,0) -- (3.4,0) node[below] {$x$};
\draw[{Stealth}-{Stealth}] (0,-3.4) -- (0,3.4) node[left] {$y$};
\draw[thick,{Stealth}-{Stealth}] (2,-3.2) -- (2,3.2);
\node[above] at (2,3.3) {$x=k$};
\fill (2,0) circle (2pt);
\node[anchor=north east] at (1.92,-0.08) {$k$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

