workedExamples.solution_text:
(a) Every point on the horizontal line has $y$-coordinate $-4$, so its equation is $y=-4$. There is no rise, so its gradient is $0$.

(b) Every point on the vertical line has $x$-coordinate $2$, so its equation is $x=2$. The run is $0$, so its gradient is undefined.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=6mm,y=6mm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\draw[help lines,color={rgb,255:red,204;green,204;blue,204},xstep=6mm,ystep=6mm] (-5,-5) grid (5,5);
\draw[<->,black] (-5.4,0)--(5.4,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[<->,black] (0,-5.4)--(0,5.4) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[black,thick,<->] (-5,-4)--(5,-4);
\draw[black,thick,<->] (2,-5)--(2,5);
\fill[black] (0,-4) circle (1.5pt);
\fill[black] (2,0) circle (1.5pt);
\node[above left] at (0,-4) {$(0,-4)$};
\node[above right] at (2,0) {$(2,0)$};
\node[below] at (-2.5,-4) {$y=-4$};
\node[right] at (2,3) {$x=2$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

workedExamples.question_text:
Write the equation of the line through $P(4,-2)$ that is:

(a) parallel to the $x$-axis;

(b) parallel to the $y$-axis.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=6mm,y=6mm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\draw[help lines,color={rgb,255:red,204;green,204;blue,204},xstep=6mm,ystep=6mm] (-5,-5) grid (5,5);
\draw[<->,black] (-5.4,0)--(5.4,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[<->,black] (0,-5.4)--(0,5.4) node[above] {$y$};
\fill[black] (4,-2) circle (1.5pt);
\node[above left] at (4,-2) {$P(4,-2)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

workedExamples.solution_text:
(a) Parallel to the $x$-axis means horizontal. Every point has the same $y$-coordinate as $P$, so the equation is $y=-2$.

(b) Parallel to the $y$-axis means vertical. Every point has the same $x$-coordinate as $P$, so the equation is $x=4$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=6mm,y=6mm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\draw[help lines,color={rgb,255:red,204;green,204;blue,204},xstep=6mm,ystep=6mm] (-5,-5) grid (5,5);
\draw[<->,black] (-5.4,0)--(5.4,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[<->,black] (0,-5.4)--(0,5.4) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[black,thick,<->] (-5,-2)--(5,-2);
\draw[black,thick,<->] (4,-5)--(4,5);
\fill[black] (4,-2) circle (1.5pt);
\node[below left] at (4,-2) {$P(4,-2)$};
\node[above] at (-2.5,-2) {$y=-2$};
\node[left] at (4,3) {$x=4$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

workedExamples.question_text:
Write the equation and state the gradient of:

(a) the $x$-axis;

(b) the $y$-axis.

workedExamples.solution_text:
(a) Every point on the $x$-axis has $y$-coordinate $0$. Its equation is $y=0$. It is horizontal, so its gradient is $0$.

(b) Every point on the $y$-axis has $x$-coordinate $0$. Its equation is $x=0$. It is vertical, so its gradient is undefined.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=6mm,y=6mm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\draw[black,thick,<->] (-5,0)--(5,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[black,thick,<->] (0,-4)--(0,4) node[above] {$y$};
\node[above] at (3,0) {$y=0$};
\node[right] at (0,2.5) {$x=0$};
\node[below left] at (0,0) {$(0,0)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

The vertical sides are $x=-3$ and $x=2$. The horizontal sides are $y=-2$ and $y=4$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=6mm,y=6mm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\draw[<->,black] (-4.5,0)--(4,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[<->,black] (0,-3.5)--(0,5) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[black,thick,<->] (-3,-3)--(-3,4.8);
\draw[black,thick,<->] (2,-3)--(2,4.8);
\draw[black,thick,<->] (-4,-2)--(3.5,-2);
\draw[black,thick,<->] (-4,4)--(3.5,4);
\node[above] at (-3,4.8) {$x=-3$};
\node[above] at (2,4.8) {$x=2$};
\node[right] at (3.5,4) {$y=4$};
\node[right] at (3.5,-2) {$y=-2$};
\draw[black,<->] (-3,-3.2)--(2,-3.2) node[midway,below] {$5$};
\draw[black,<->] (-4.2,-2)--(-4.2,4) node[midway,left] {$6$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Width: $2-(-3)=5$ units.

Height: $4-(-2)=6$ units.

$\begin{align*}A&=5\times6\\&=30\end{align*}$

The area is $30$ square units.
